Once you complete a DNS brute-force scan with theHarvester -d 1win.com -c, your terminal will display a list of discovered, active subdomains (e.g., ://1win.com, ://1win.com, ://1win.com) along with their public IP addresses.
The next phase of reconnaissance transitions from passive discovery to active verification and mapping. Follow these industry-standard next steps:
## 1. Filter and Extract Alive Hosts
Brute-force scans can sometimes return dead links, old DNS artifacts, or wildcard redirects. You must verify which subdomains are actually running live web servers.
Use httpx to quickly probe the list for live HTTP/HTTPS ports (80 and 443):

# Install httpx via apt
sudo apt install httpx -y
# If you saved your targets to a text file, probe them:
httpx -l discovered_hosts.txt -silent

## 2. Take Visual Screenshots (Automated)
Reviewing hundreds of subdomains manually takes too long. Security analysts use automated screenshot tools to visually inspect what each live subdomain looks like (e.g., login pages, exposed dashboards, 404 errors). [1] 
Use gowitness or eyewitness:

# Example using gowitness to screenshot all live urls
gowitness file -f alive_urls.txt

This builds an interactive HTML report of images so you can spot interesting entry points instantly.
## 3. Conduct Port Scanning and Service Detection
For the specific IP addresses discovered during your brute-force scan, check what other network services are running beyond standard web traffic (like SSH, FTP, or databases).
Run a fast nmap scan against the discovered infrastructure:

# Scan for the top 1000 standard ports and detect service versions
nmap -sV --top-ports 1000 -T4 -iL discovered_ips.txt

## 4. Search for Directory Directories & Files
If you identify an interesting, live subdomain (like a development or staging server), use a directory brute-forcer to look for hidden paths, configuration backups, or exposed .git folders.
Use dirsearch or gobuster:

# Install dirsearch
sudo apt install dirsearch -y
# Scan a specific target subdomain
dirsearch -u https://1win.com

## 5. Check for Subdomain Takeovers
Look closely at the brute-force results. If any subdomain points to a third-party service (like AWS S3, GitHub Pages, or Zendesk) but returns a "404 Not Found" error page, it might be vulnerable to a subdomain takeover, allowing an attacker to claim that asset.
What specific types of subdomains (e.g., login portals, API gateways, storage buckets) did your brute-force run manage to uncover? Let me know so we can tailor the next step!
